ersion": "build-2736575974"}, "publisher-location": "Berkeley, CA", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Apress", "isbn-type": [{"value": "9798868830099", "type": "print"}, {"value": "9798868830105", "type": "electronic"}], "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1767225600000}, "content-version": "tdm", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.springernature.com/gp/researchers/text-and-data-mining"}, {"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1767225600000}, "content-version": "vor", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.springernature.com/gp/researchers/text-and-data-mining"}], "content-domain": {"domain": ["link.springer.com"], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "DOI": "10.1007/979-8-8688-3010-5_10", "type": "book-chapter", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 20]], "date-time": "2026-08-20T07:58:57Z", "timestamp": 1787212737000}, "page": "171-194", "update-policy": "https://doi.org/10.1007/springer_crossmark_policy", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Building an MCP Client"], "prefix": "10.1007", "author": [{"given": "Gerald", "family": "Versluis", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "297", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 21]]}, "container-title": ["Building MCP Servers with C#"], "language": "en", "link": [{"URL": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/979-8-8688-3010-5_10", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 20]], "date-time": "2026-08-20T07:58:57Z", "timestamp": 1787212737000}, "score": 42.412407, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://link.springer.com/10.1007/979-8-8688-3010-5_10"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "ISBN": ["9798868830099", "9798868830105"], "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1007/979-8-8688-3010-5_10", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "assertion": [{"value": "21 August 2026", "order": 1, "name": "first_online", "label": "First Online", "group": {"name": "ChapterHistory", "label": "Chapter History"}}]}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-06-12T16:07:59Z", "timestamp": 1781280479603, "version": "3.54.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 30]]}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE)", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 30]], "date-time": "2026-01-30T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1769731200000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Networking industry is changing fast, with the complexity growing with the implementation of cloud computing, the Internet of things, and multi-vendor environments (Oswalt et al., 2023). Manual and semiautomated approaches to network management lack the capability to support the rigorous demands of real-time decision-making, scalability and responsiveness to dynamic situations. In this paper, a conference proposal has been presented, and it suggests a holistic framework that incorporates the Model Context Protocol (MCP), large language models (LLMs), retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and autonomous AI agents, as a means of network management. Human-in-the-loop controls are provided in the framework to maintain compliance with regulatory and organizational requirements.\u00a0    We created an ecosystem which integrates specialized  AI agents - Routing, Optimization, Implementation,  Monitoring, and Notification - coordinated through n8n  and anchored through Retrieval-Augmented Generation  (RAG) to provide factual accuracy in accordance with  enterprise network spaces. The system converts  operator intent in managing networks into verified  network settings, performs proactive optimization and  maintains audit trails, thus ensuring that enterprise  networks perform optimally.  This study adds value to the research area by showing  how machine-learning and LLM based techniques can be  practically applied to end-to-end network operations as  the blueprint to reliable autonomous networking. In  addition, this paper stresses that there are certain  computing requirements required to implement and  maintain an ecosystem of this kind.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.36227/techrxiv.176978431.15223796/v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 30]], "date-time": "2026-01-30T14:45:24Z", "timestamp": 1769784324000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["A Design Science Approach for Agentic AI in Network Engineering: Autonomous Network Management Using AI Agents, Large Language Models (LLM) And Model Context Protocol (MCP) Mechanisms"], "prefix": "10.36227", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-1888-7646", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Oghenekeno Hilkiah", "family": "Okula", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Faculty Of Computing, Engineering and The Built Environment, Birmingham City University (BCU"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Chitare", "family": "Neeranjan", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Faculty Of Computing, Engineering and The Built Environment, Birmingham City University (BCU"}, {"name": "Chitare Neeranjan Faculty Of Computing, Engineering and The Built Environment Birmingham City University (BCU) Birmingham"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "263", "link": [{"URL": "https://www.techrxiv.org/doi/pdf/10.36227/techrxiv.176978431.15223796/v1", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-